PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.6/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A coherent, well-documented 120-level campaign. Most levels have one readable aha, an intended plan and a named trap, and the planning forecast makes the traps fair. The weaknesses are structural. The same aha shape repeats in about 60% of levels: shorten the line or split it, then count hops against seats. Breathers fall on a fixed 12-level cadence and are weakly differentiated. A few levels are cluttered or have a muddy aha (L33, L62, L67, L94, L55). The tone is also more arithmetic than the "calm, gentle" positioning suggests. It sits below Royal Match and Flow Free in variety and below Monument Valley in visual surprise, but clearly above generic puzzle sets for teaching order. Curve: Pacing is disciplined. Each city introduces one mechanic alone (tunnel L13, carriage L25, hill L37, ferry L49, snow L61, interchange L73, loop L85, wave L97, heritage L109), practises it, combines it with earlier rules, then runs boss, breather and a gentle mastery level. Mechanics are never stacked before they are taught. The first 12 levels are strong, and the casual win rates (L1-L3 at 87-97%) meet the targets. Problems: (1) The 12-level rhythm is too regular, so players can predict the breather and boss slots and the trap formula ("the tidy option loses"). (2) The aha shape repeats too often: split into short lines, check hops and seats, find the trap that is one long line. Later cities need more variety, such as a level where the answer is to do nothing, or one where the obvious build is correct. L46, L32 and L58 show this works. (3) Breathers L7, L19, L43, L55, L67 and L91 all read "any network that reaches every stop wins", with a drawing puzzle for the 3rd star. Vary their texture. (4) Difficulty: late casual rates of 44-60% are on target. L62 (73% casual, 77% skilled) is the one anomaly, because the gap suggests an undiscoverable aha. L90 (44% casual) after L89 (63%) is a noticeable spike. L120 (39%) is acceptable for a finale. (5) Bosses in the second half lean on forecast reading and card choice, which suits the calm pillars well. Overall the order is good, but the middle needs more variety to avoid fatigue.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L33 (6/10): Two 5-cell empty vertical corridors waste board and read as clutter; the F-G tail is dead space on screen. → Compact the lane to 1-2 spacings or give each stop along it a rider so the corridor earns its length.
L55 (6/10): Third 'optional crossing, anything wins' breather (L19, L55); 93%/99% with no puzzle beyond not skipping a stop. → Give it a distinct 3rd-star puzzle (for example, serve the lotus piers with exactly one boat and no change) or fold into a different texture.
L62 (6/10): Casual 73% and skilled 77% are nearly equal, so skilled players do not find the aha; the correct reading (make M a line end) is hidden behind the familiar shuttle reflex, a guess in practice. → Surface it: show M's riders split by destination in the preview and a hint card, or shrink the street so the shuttle trap fails visibly in week 1.
L67 (6/10): Aha text contradicts itself (the long walk 'misses the 2nd star delivering more riders'); star logic is unclear and the 3rd star is a tiny hop-count win. → Define stars cleanly (all riders delivered, no amber, 1 line) and make the short-side choice the only difference.
L94 (6/10): Near copy of L85/L89 (close the ring, leave a silent kiosk off); the budget idea adds little. → Replace with a board where closing the loop costs a track that would otherwise serve a loaded stop.
L7 (7/10): Fine, but the decorative pond comma-grid adds nothing and the puzzle is a plain Hamiltonian-path exercise. → Make the pond river a real visual and put a second tempting dead end, so the path choice has a second false answer.
L8 (7/10): Aha is 'skip B', yet B is a zero-rider decoy and the board is a near copy of L4/L6 construction-site logic. → Give B a small but real demand that pulls the player to include it, so skipping is a real trade-off.
L13 (7/10): Teaches tunnel while also leaning on hop arithmetic and a spur. Casual 90% is the first weak-aha level of the new mechanic. → Strip to one question: two crossing spots, one tunnel, a single heavy pair, no extra Kite spur.
L16 (7/10): Three rules at once right after tunnels are barely practised; the card choice is guessable from demand numbers. → Move after L18 or drop the late quay E so the card choice is the only decision.
L19 (7/10): Same 'anything wins' template as L7; 93%/100% adds no tension and the 3rd-star shortcut is obvious. → Make the breather a different texture, for example a pure aesthetic route where the star is a symmetry or scenic-line goal.
L21 (7/10): Layout has a long empty vertical corridor and the land-bridge trap is obvious; 8 tracks make it forgiving. → Shorten the board and cut one track so the bridge option is tempting but costly.
L24 (7/10): Either card wins and casual 93% makes it a soft send-off with a thin aha ('build nothing'). → Make the calm card visibly pay off, for example a small queue that only the unspent track can clear.
L31 (7/10): Two correct answers, 92% casual; the one-line puzzle repeats L7. → Offer a distinct 3rd-star goal, such as a loop-free symmetric line, rather than 'at most 1 line'.
L36 (7/10): One-line board with an obvious fix card; the +2 tracks option is a strawman. → Make +2 tracks look attractive, such as an unreached stop that a track could fetch.
L41 (7/10): Skilled 89% yet the aha is a single path with a very obvious hop count; the 'quiet star C can wait' omission echoes L17/L39. → Give C a modest demand so omitting it has a visible cost.
L43 (7/10): Same 'one line' drawing puzzle again; the 0-rider B and E stops are filler. → Replace with a visual-aesthetic puzzle, such as drawing a symmetric figure, and remove the dead stops.
L60 (7/10): Optional isle G is a weak 3rd star, and the aha repeats L51/L57. → Make G's riders tight on the east boat so the player must count, not just add it.
L64 (7/10): Casual 61% for a level that is mostly L49 with halved seats; the aha is a re-run. → Add a second demand that makes the snow change which crossing is best, or fold into L65-style.
L68 (7/10): C has 1 rider and the diamond layout is cramped; two correct ways to pair stops. → Give C and E more demand so the climb line is the only heavy path, and tidy the layout.
L79 (7/10): The upgrade is the only decision and it is obvious (only one stop is loaded); long empty arms waste space. → Shorten the arms and put a rival loaded stop so the interchange target must be read.
L84 (7/10): Several cards win and the line-card nuance (spend or lose) is fiddly. → Make the line card unambiguously worse, or give a visible bonus for the interchange read.
L88 (7/10): The hill rail is never used, so it is decoy clutter; skilled 79% suggests the late closing is non-obvious. → Make the hill rail a real alternative with a visible cost, or drop it and teach only the leave-a-gap idea.
L91 (7/10): A T-shaped board with 8 tracks and 91% casual; the choice is trivial. → Give the pier line a real direction choice with a measurable star difference.
L103 (7/10): Reruns L70's idea on a tiny board; not clearly easier than neighbours' difficulty (79%). → Raise breather ease (more tracks, softer goal) or make it the 'no loops' texture breather it claims.
L108 (7/10): Several answers win and the mastery is mild; 'loop partly right' lacks a sharp aha. → Add a decoy second triangle so the player must pick which to close.

level-design score 7.6/10 — A coherent, well-documented 120-level campaign. Most levels have one readable aha, an intended plan and a named trap, and the planning forecast makes the traps fair. The weaknesses are structural. The same aha shape repeats in about 60% of levels: shorten the line or split it, then count hops against seats. Breathers fall on a fixed 12-level cadence and are weakly differentiated. A few levels are cluttered or have a muddy aha (L33, L62, L67, L94, L55). The tone is also more arithmetic than the "calm, gentle" positioning suggests. It sits below Royal Match and Flow Free in variety and below Monument Valley in visual surprise, but clearly above generic puzzle sets for teaching order.
curve: Pacing is disciplined. Each city introduces one mechanic alone (tunnel L13, carriage L25, hill L37, ferry L49, snow L61, interchange L73, loop L85, wave L97, heritage L109), practises it, combines it with earlier rules, then runs boss, breather and a gentle mastery level. Mechanics are never stacked before they are taught. The first 12 levels are strong, and the casual win rates (L1-L3 at 87-97%) meet the targets. Problems: (1) The 12-level rhythm is too regular, so players can predict the breather and boss slots and the trap formula ("the tidy option loses"). (2) The aha shape repeats too often: split into short lines, check hops and seats, find the trap that is one long line. Later cities need more variety, such as a level where the answer is to do nothing, or one where the obvious build is correct. L46, L32 and L58 show this works. (3) Breathers L7, L19, L43, L55, L67 and L91 all read "any network that reaches every stop wins", with a drawing puzzle for the 3rd star. Vary their texture. (4) Difficulty: late casual rates of 44-60% are on target. L62 (73% casual, 77% skilled) is the one anomaly, because the gap suggests an undiscoverable aha. L90 (44% casual) after L89 (63%) is a noticeable spike. L120 (39%) is acceptable for a finale. (5) Bosses in the second half lean on forecast reading and card choice, which suits the calm pillars well. Overall the order is good, but the middle needs more variety to avoid fatigue.
